<Entry
  slug="ucla-football-ai-assistant"
  image="/img/ucla-dashboard.jpg"
  links={[{ href: 'https://github.com/mouchatt6/UCLA-Football-Strength-App-BSA-2026', label: 'Team repo (code only)' }]}
>
  <h2><span class="label">The question</span>Can a strength coach just <em>ask</em> the data?</h2>
  <p>
    UCLA Football’s strength staff track every athlete through seven data feeds: four VALD force-plate
    devices, Catapult GPS, SmartSpeed sprint timing, and a hand-kept Google Sheet. Bruin Sports Analytics
    maintains the dashboard that pulls it together. It has percentiles, an “Athleticism Score,” and
    workload-ratio injury flags. But every new question still meant someone clicking through tabs, or asking
    an analyst.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was asked to scope whether a natural-language assistant was feasible. The answer turned out to be a
    working one.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="label">What I built</span>A tool-using assistant inside the dashboard</h2>
  <Note>Answers in ~6 s. Opus at low effort beat the cheaper model, which got facts wrong in eval.</Note>
  <p>
    The assistant is a new tab in the R Shiny app. A coach types something like <em>“Who on the defensive
    line improved their jump most this month?”</em> Claude (via the Anthropic API) doesn’t guess. It calls
    one of <mark>17 R functions</mark> I wrote that query the real data: screen players, compare over time,
    derive a metric, check who is overdue for testing, or build a branded HTML report to download. For
    anything those don’t cover, there is a fallback where the model writes an allow-listed dplyr pipeline
    and R runs it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A metric dictionary tells the model what each of 60+ measurements means and which direction is “better.”</li>
    <li>Answers come back as a chart, a table, or a CSV, not just a paragraph.</li>
    <li>An eval of <mark>32 coach-style questions</mark> with factual checks runs before changes ship. All checks pass.</li>
  </ul>

  <Plate src="/img/ucla-dashboard.jpg" alt="The UCLA Performance Database dashboard showing a roster table ranked by Athleticism Score" fig="1" wide>
    The dashboard the assistant lives in, running on <strong>synthetic data</strong>. Every name and number here is
    fake. Real athlete data never leaves the private data repo.
  </Plate>

  <h2><span class="label">Why it’s careful</span>Built on a pipeline with a history</h2>
  <p>
    An assistant that answers confidently from bad data is worse than none. When our team took over the
    pipeline, it came with a data-integrity audit from the previous maintainers. That audit had found six of
    106 hand-entered rows overwritten with the wrong athlete’s name, an error that sat
    <mark>undetected for about six months</mark>. So the assistant only ever reads through the same
    validated, identity-matched data the dashboard uses.
  </p>
  <Specimen rows={[
    ['Data feeds', '7'],
    ['Assistant tools', '17'],
    ['Eval questions', '32, all checks pass'],
    ['Median answer time', '~6 s'],
  ]} />

  <h2><span class="label">Status</span>Where it stands</h2>
  <p>
    The assistant is a working demo on synthetic data. Using it on real athletes is waiting on UCLA’s privacy
    sign-off for sending data to an external model. One option we’re proposing is pseudonymizing names to IDs
    before anything leaves the app.
  </p>
</Entry>
